you do not accept it, do not use the Software.

The Software is proprietary.  It is licensed, not sold.  Except for the
rights granted below, all rights are reserved by the copyright holder.

Two things this Agreement does NOT restrict, stated up front because they
are what most readers came here for:

  * It costs nothing, for any use, including commercial use.  See §1.
  * It claims nothing in the programs you compile with it.  See §4.


1. Grant of Use
---------------

Subject to this Agreement, you are granted a non-exclusive, worldwide,
royalty-free license to install and use the Software, free of charge, for
any purpose, including commercial purposes, on any number of machines,
by any number of people within your organization.

No fee, registration, license key, usage report, or attribution is
required to use the Software.


2. Redistribution of the Unmodified Artifact
--------------------------------------------

You may copy and redistribute the Software, free of charge, provided that
you redistribute it **unmodified** — byte-for-byte as published, as a
complete release artifact, with this Agreement, LICENSE-APACHE, and
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md included and unaltered.

This permission exists so that mirrors, package caches, continuous-
integration caches, container images, internal artifact repositories, and
offline installers can hold a copy without asking.  You may charge for
the medium, bandwidth, or service that delivers it; you may not charge
for the license itself.

You may not redistribute a modified artifact, a partial artifact, or a
repackaging that presents the Software as your own.


3. Restrictions
---------------

Except to the extent this Agreement permits it, or a license in
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md permits it (see §8), or applicable law grants you
a right that cannot be waived by contract, you may not:

  (a) modify, adapt, translate, or create derivative works of the
      Software's binaries;

  (b) reverse engineer, decompile, or disassemble the Software's
      binaries, or otherwise attempt to derive their source code;

  (c) remove, obscure, or alter any copyright, license, or attribution
      notice in or accompanying the Software;

  (d) sublicense, rent, lease, or sell the Software, or offer it as a
      hosted service in which the Software itself is the product being
      sold;

  (e) use the Software's name or the copyright holder's name to endorse
      or promote your products (see §7).

These restrictions apply to the Software's binaries.  They do not apply
to the source files the Software ships alongside those binaries — the C
runtime sources under `bin/` and the components identified in §4 — which
carry their own licenses and are meant to be read, modified, and compiled.


4. Compiler Output — What You Build Is Yours
--------------------------------------------

**This section prevails over every other section of this Agreement.**

A compiler is a tool.  The project claims no interest in what you make
with it.  Specifically:

  (a) **You own your output.**  Programs, libraries, object files,
      executables, WebAssembly modules, firmware images, and any other
      artifact produced by compiling, assembling, linking, or running
      your source code with the Software are yours.  This Agreement
      grants the copyright holder no interest in them of any kind.

  (b) **No license is required to distribute them.**  You may use, sell,
      distribute, sublicense, keep secret, or open-source your compiler
      output on any terms you choose.  You need no permission from the
      novo-lang project, owe it no royalty, and owe it no attribution.

  (c) **This includes the parts of novo-lang that end up inside your
      binaries.**  A compiled novo-lang program statically links the
      novo-lang C runtime and contains code generated from the
      novo-lang standard library.  Those components are licensed to
      the public under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (see
      LICENSE-APACHE), and their
      presence in your binary creates no obligation to the novo-lang project.

  (d) **How the Apache-2.0 obligation is discharged.**  Apache-2.0 §4
      would ordinarily require a redistributor of those components to
      pass along a copy of the license and any NOTICE file.  Two things
      dispose of that here:

        1. The copyright holder is the sole licensor of the novo-lang runtime
           and standard library.  There is no third-party NOTICE content
           in them to propagate: the attribution notice for those
           components is the one the Software itself ships, in
           LICENSE-APACHE and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, in the toolchain
           artifact you already have.  No NOTICE file travels with
           compiled output because there is nothing in it that the
           artifact does not already carry.

        2. To remove any doubt, the copyright holder grants an
           additional permission, as permitted by Apache-2.0 §4: **you
           may distribute compiler output containing the novo-lang runtime
           and standard library without satisfying any condition of
           Apache-2.0 §4** — no license copy, no NOTICE file, no
           attribution, no marking of modified files.  This permission
           is irrevocable and runs with the output.

      If you would rather rely on Apache-2.0 directly for those
      components, you may; this permission is offered in addition to it,
      not in place of it, and it takes nothing away.

  (e) **Your source is your source.**  Writing novo-lang source code, or
      compiling it, does not place it under this Agreement or any other
      license of the project's choosing.


